{
  "teamId": 145,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Chicago White Sox entered the season with cautious optimism, but April quickly revealed the hard truth: this was a team that would spend most of the year fighting a losing battle. A brutal 7-20 April set the tone, as Reynaldo López became an unwilling symbol of the rotation's fragility, surrendering multiple game-defining home runs while the offense sputtered. Bright moments — Andrew Vaughn's early fireworks, a late-April rally — were overwhelmed by the sheer volume of losses piling up on the South Side.\n\nMay offered a brief, tantalizing reprieve. The Sox went 15-14, their only winning month of the season, buoyed by clutch hits from Andrew Benintendi, Luis Robert Jr., and Gavin Sheets. For a few weeks it seemed possible that the roster's talent might cohere into something competitive. But the bullpen remained a liability, and the underlying run differential — surrendering 841 runs on the season — made the May record feel like a mirage rather than a foundation.\n\nJune and July told the real story. The team went a combined 20-29 over those two months, and the high-leverage play log reads like a catalogue of heartbreak: Kendall Graveman coughing up leads, field errors extending innings, and opponents launching grand slams at the worst possible moments. Luis Robert Jr. was a genuine star, delivering a Fourth of July three-run blast that briefly lit up the South Side, but the surrounding cast couldn't sustain any momentum. By the All-Star break, the postseason was a distant memory.\n\nAugust and September were a slow, painful descent into a 101-loss season. Gregory Santos emerged as the latest reliever to torment his own team, surrendering a three-run walk-off homer in mid-August that encapsulated the year's cruelest pattern. Robert kept slugging — his 38th home run in late September was a lonely monument to individual brilliance amid collective failure. The White Sox closed the books on 2023 as one of baseball's most disappointing teams, a roster brimming with individual talent that never found the cohesion to compete.",
